WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.270 --> 00:00:02.330
Bronte: Good afternoon.

00:00:02.330 --> 00:00:07.800
It is my pleasure to introduce Mr. Richard
Brookhiser, a graduate of Yale University.

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:12.300
Mr. Brookhiser is senior editor for National
Review where he has worked since 1977.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:18.150
He has written for numerous other publications
including The New York Observer and the The

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:19.150
Atlantic.

00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:23.191
He is also the biographer and historian of
the Founding Period, and the author of several

00:00:23.191 --> 00:00:28.270
books including Rediscovering George Washington,
and most recently, Founders' Son: A Life of

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:29.890
Abraham Lincoln.

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:35.660
He was awarded in 2008 the National Medal
of the Humanities, and in 2011 he received

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:37.700
a Guggenheim fellowship.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:46.700
Please join me in welcoming Mr. Richard Brookhiser.

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:54.920
Richard: Thank you.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:55.920
Thank you, Bronte.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:57.170
Thank you, Matt.

00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:02.649
Thank you all for coming out here in this,
I guess, typical south central Michigan winter

00:01:02.649 --> 00:01:03.780
day.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:06.770
I'm very pleased to be back at Hillsdale.

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:09.649
This is my fourth time here.

00:01:09.649 --> 00:01:16.639
It's always good to be here and good to see
so many old friends, but particularly for

00:01:16.639 --> 00:01:21.409
a journalism conference, my old friend and
colleague, Professor John Miller.

00:01:21.409 --> 00:01:24.560
Are there students of his here in the journalism
program?

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:26.229
Yes, okay.

00:01:26.229 --> 00:01:29.639
Be like him and you'll do fine.

00:01:29.639 --> 00:01:32.219
That's my tip.

00:01:32.219 --> 00:01:38.859
I'm going to talk this afternoon about journalism
and the Founding Era.

00:01:38.859 --> 00:01:47.829
Journalism can do a lot for good and for ill,
and in Founding Era America they sought all.

00:01:47.829 --> 00:01:55.700
I'm going to talk about four journalists who
illustrate four aspects of journalism.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:02:05.299
Those aspects are attacking, amusing, instructing,
and inspiring.

00:02:05.299 --> 00:02:15.900
The four journalists who show these qualities
are James Callender, attack; Benjamin Franklin,

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:25.450
amusement; Alexander Hamilton, instruction;
and Thomas Payne, inspiration.

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:30.640
Before I get to my journalists, I just have
to explain a little bit about the world in

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:33.360
which they operated.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:38.540
When we're talking about 18th century journalism
we're talking about print.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:44.250
Telegraphs and photographs were not developed
until the 1840s, and obviously everything

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:47.310
wireless is 20th century.

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:50.440
The 18th century does have print.

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:59.350
It has newspapers, and it also has pamphlets
which are freestanding articles, cheaper to

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:01.280
produce than books.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:06.760
You could think of them as magazine assays
without the magazine, although some of them

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:10.310
can be quite lengthy.

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:15.000
America in the 18th century had an usually
high number of newspapers.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:21.170
I'm not going to give you an exact figure
because the figure keeps growing throughout

00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:28.290
the period, but think in terms of several
dozen and constantly expanding.

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:33.310
The interesting figure is the contrast with
other countries.

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:39.080
American has more newspapers per capita in
the 18th century than Britain and many more

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:44.630
per capita than France even though Britain
and France are the two super powers in the

00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:50.410
world and we are a small provincial ex-colonial
country.

00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:53.750
How did we get so many of them?

00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:55.940
Part of it's geography.

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:59.430
Our population is small, but it's very spread
out.

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:06.400
It's strung out along the east coast, and
then it begins to move into the interior.

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:10.110
There's no equivalent to London or Paris.

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:13.690
No metropolis that sucks people into it.

00:04:13.690 --> 00:04:18.500
The population is disbursed, and therefore
the newspapers are disbursed.

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:23.639
His population is also surprisingly literate.

00:04:23.639 --> 00:04:26.850
The ultimate reason for that is probably religious.

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:34.280
There were a number of churches in colonial
America which were very keen on Bible reading.

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:41.840
If people were educated to be able to read
a Bible they were also able to read a newspaper,

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:46.659
so there was a large potential audience there.

00:04:46.659 --> 00:04:53.030
The founding fathers were very aware of this
media world.

00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:59.479
A number of them were part-time or full-time
professional journalists.

00:04:59.479 --> 00:05:02.720
Sam Adams published a newspaper.

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:08.279
Benjamin Franklin for much of his life was
a printer who published several different

00:05:08.279 --> 00:05:09.379
newspaper.

00:05:09.379 --> 00:05:14.581
At the end of his life, Alexander Hamilton
founds and publishes a newspaper, The New

00:05:14.581 --> 00:05:20.250
York Evening Post, which is still around as
The New York Post.

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:27.210
Thomas Jefferson and James Madison when Jefferson
was secretary of state and Madison was a congressman,

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:32.240
they start a newspaper in Philadelphia which
is the nation's capital.

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:38.300
Neither Jefferson nor Madison actually edits
the paper, but Madison finds a man to edit

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:43.909
it, and Jefferson gives him a part-time job
in the State Department to guarantee his income

00:05:43.909 --> 00:05:52.389
so it'll be easier for him to bring out this
newspaper that they want to get up and running.

00:05:52.389 --> 00:05:58.690
Almost all the founding fathers write for
newspaper or write pamphlets at one time or

00:05:58.690 --> 00:06:03.249
another, and all of them read newspapers.

00:06:03.249 --> 00:06:11.430
One visitor to Mount Vernon noticed that George
Washington had subscriptions to 10 newspapers.

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:15.629
These men are very aware of this world, very
involved in it.

00:06:15.629 --> 00:06:20.620
One other point about it is that all the newspapers
have a point of view.

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:26.940
They all express some sort of partisan opinion
and no one is bothered by that.

00:06:26.940 --> 00:06:31.740
They will disagree with that point of view,
but no one thinks wouldn't it be nice if they're

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:33.259
all objective.

00:06:33.259 --> 00:06:35.539
That's just not a goal.

00:06:35.539 --> 00:06:41.620
You want your newspaper to be informative
because that makes it more interesting and

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:49.030
more useful, but everybody expects every newspaper
to be expressing a particular point of view.

00:06:49.030 --> 00:06:51.580
That is the world.

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:54.199
That's the journalism.

00:06:54.199 --> 00:06:59.490
James Callender enters this world from Scotland.

00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:05.830
He starts off as a young man in Scotland writing
radical pamphlets after the beginning of the

00:07:05.830 --> 00:07:09.080
French Revolution in 1789.

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:16.000
This causes a ferment throughout the Atlantic
world, and Britain very soon cracks down on

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:21.969
it because they don't want revolutionary ideas
spreading to Britain.

00:07:21.969 --> 00:07:27.669
Callender decides in the early 1790s it would
be better and safer for him to move to the

00:07:27.669 --> 00:07:29.689
United States.

00:07:29.689 --> 00:07:34.999
He moves to Philadelphia where he reports
on debates in Congress.

00:07:34.999 --> 00:07:39.650
There he meets the Clerk of the House who's
a man named John Beckley.

00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:47.979
Now at this time, the early 17902, America
is developing its first two-party system which

00:07:47.979 --> 00:07:56.089
will be the Federalist of Alexander Hamilton
versus the Republicans of Jefferson and Madison.

00:07:56.089 --> 00:08:00.729
The Republicans would later become the Democrats,
the Democratic Party of today, the Modern

00:08:00.729 --> 00:08:06.219
Republican Party has nothing to do with either
of these parties.

00:08:06.219 --> 00:08:14.569
When we think of the disputes that Hamilton
on the one hand had with Jefferson and Madison,

00:08:14.569 --> 00:08:23.199
we think of it as a battle of titans, of founding
fathers disagreeing about financial policy,

00:08:23.199 --> 00:08:29.060
about the French Revolution, about the size
and structure of government.

00:08:29.060 --> 00:08:31.249
That's true.

00:08:31.249 --> 00:08:39.140
Below these great men there was a sub-world,
almost and underworld, of assistants, helpers,

00:08:39.140 --> 00:08:45.890
gossips, snoops, errand boys, journalists.

00:08:45.890 --> 00:08:48.890
John Beckley is part of that world.

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:57.750
He works for Jefferson and Madison, and he
spots Callender as a recruit for their side.

00:08:57.750 --> 00:09:03.380
Callender's first great scoop happens in 1797.

00:09:03.380 --> 00:09:08.410
He writes a pamphlet called A History of the
United States for the year 1796.

00:09:08.410 --> 00:09:17.050
I wish I could quote from it, but it's impossible
because no copies of it exist.

00:09:17.050 --> 00:09:22.350
It was ferocious attack on Alexander Hamilton,
and it seems as if Hamilton and his family

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:27.370
bought up every copy they could find, so none
are now left.

00:09:27.370 --> 00:09:34.330
It made a big splash when it came out because
Callender made the charge that Hamilton, he

00:09:34.330 --> 00:09:42.140
was now retired, but he was Treasury Secretary
he was engaged in insider trading with a small-time

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:44.990
crook named James Reynolds.

00:09:44.990 --> 00:09:49.440
Hamilton would tip him off about Treasury
policies before they were announced.

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:56.470
Reynolds would go out and make trades, and
Hamilton paid Reynolds for these services.

00:09:56.470 --> 00:10:04.060
In order to defend himself, Hamilton wrote
a 95-page pamphlet called Observations on

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:10.100
Certain Documents where he admitted that he
knew James Reynolds.

00:10:10.100 --> 00:10:16.300
He admitted that he had given Reynolds $1,000
during one year when he was Treasury Secretary,

00:10:16.300 --> 00:10:21.540
but the reason he paid him was not because
of insider trading but because he was having

00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:28.210
an affair with Maria Reynolds, James' wife,
and James Reynolds had blackmailed him.

00:10:28.210 --> 00:10:35.100
To prove this, he printed Maria's love letters,
James' blackmail letters, and an affidavit

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:42.080
from Maria's former landlord who remembered
her complaining to him that her husband wanted

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:48.410
to pimp her to prominent men, and Hamilton
was the man they caught.

00:10:48.410 --> 00:10:51.810
This obviously created a sensation.

00:10:51.810 --> 00:10:53.740
It thrilled Hamilton's enemies.

00:10:53.740 --> 00:10:56.520
It mortified his allies.

00:10:56.520 --> 00:11:01.610
He protected his reputation for financial
integrity.

00:11:01.610 --> 00:11:06.540
His reputation for common sense suffered.

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:08.730
After this, Callender kept writing.

00:11:08.730 --> 00:11:10.930
He got in various scrapes.

00:11:10.930 --> 00:11:17.250
Then, in 1800, Thomas Jefferson wins a presidential
election.

00:11:17.250 --> 00:11:24.630
He had run against John Adams in 1796 and
lost, but in the election of 1800 the Republican

00:11:24.630 --> 00:11:30.930
party beats the Federalist party for the presidency,
and it also takes both Houses of Congress.

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:33.870
It is a Republican sweep.

00:11:33.870 --> 00:11:37.870
Now James Callender thinks he should be rewarded.

00:11:37.870 --> 00:11:44.950
He asked his friends and patrons, Jefferson
and Madison, if he could be a postmaster somewhere.

00:11:44.950 --> 00:11:49.060
It seems as if Jefferson had become suspicious
of Callender.

00:11:49.060 --> 00:11:54.640
It's not clear, but it is clear that Callender
did not get a postmastership.

00:11:54.640 --> 00:12:00.860
This was a mistake because Callender moved
to Richmond, switched parties, and opened

00:12:00.860 --> 00:12:04.570
a Federalist newspaper called The Richmond
Corridor.

00:12:04.570 --> 00:12:12.170
There, he made his second scoop, which I will
be able to read to you from, and you've all

00:12:12.170 --> 00:12:13.170
heard of it.

00:12:13.170 --> 00:12:17.320
You all know of this, though you may not know
the specifics.

00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:20.180
Here's how the story began.

00:12:20.180 --> 00:12:22.110
This is September 1802.

00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:29.610
"It is well known that the man whom it delighth
the people to honor keeps and for many years

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:34.810
past has kept as his concubine one of his
own slaves.

00:12:34.810 --> 00:12:37.740
Her name is Sally," Sally Hemings.

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:41.850
"The name of her eldest son is Tom.

00:12:41.850 --> 00:12:47.800
His features are said to bear a striking,
though sable, resemblance to those of the

00:12:47.800 --> 00:12:51.720
president himself," and it goes on and on.

00:12:51.720 --> 00:12:55.470
I just want to comment on that opening.

00:12:55.470 --> 00:12:57.190
Now is this garbage.

00:12:57.190 --> 00:13:04.320
This is characteristic assassination and politics
of personal destruction, but it is well written.

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:07.180
You have to understand this is well written.

00:13:07.180 --> 00:13:13.180
First, there's the Biblical allusion, whom
it delighth the people to honor.

00:13:13.180 --> 00:13:18.980
Everyone who read that would know that this
is a reference to the book os Esther which

00:13:18.980 --> 00:13:22.500
is commemorated in the Jewish holiday of Purim.

00:13:22.500 --> 00:13:28.580
The book of Esther is about Jews in captivity
in Persia.

00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:34.620
The king of Persia has a Jewish advisor named
Mordecai, and Mordecai's adopted daughter,

00:13:34.620 --> 00:13:38.370
Esther, is one of the king's consorts.

00:13:38.370 --> 00:13:44.850
The king has another advisor, Haman the Agagite,
who hates Mordecai and wants to destroy all

00:13:44.850 --> 00:13:47.810
the Jews.

00:13:47.810 --> 00:13:52.540
The king finds out about this, and he plays
a trick on Haman.

00:13:52.540 --> 00:13:59.090
He summons him and asks him what is the proper
reward for a man whom the king delighth to

00:13:59.090 --> 00:14:02.320
honor, this is the source of Callender's reference.

00:14:02.320 --> 00:14:08.060
Haman thinks he's talking himself, so he suggests
all sorts of nice things, and then the king

00:14:08.060 --> 00:14:11.430
reveals that he's actually thinking of Mordecai.

00:14:11.430 --> 00:14:16.550
Haman realizes that the wheel has turned,
and he is ultimately executed, and the Jews

00:14:16.550 --> 00:14:20.650
are given permission to kill all his supporters.

00:14:20.650 --> 00:14:27.090
What Callender is doing here, America doesn't
have king, but it does have the people, and

00:14:27.090 --> 00:14:33.090
the people have just elected Jefferson president,
so he's the man whom it delighth people to

00:14:33.090 --> 00:14:34.560
honor.

00:14:34.560 --> 00:14:40.520
What Callender is suggesting is after I get
done with him he will be like Haman.

00:14:40.520 --> 00:14:49.420
Then, he goes on, and we have this wonderful
choice of words in this sentence where he

00:14:49.420 --> 00:14:56.030
says that Sally's son, Tom, has a sable resemblance
to Jefferson.

00:14:56.030 --> 00:15:00.910
He could've said dark, which would've been
good because that would associate Jefferson

00:15:00.910 --> 00:15:04.240
with darkness, but he says sable.

00:15:04.240 --> 00:15:07.210
That gives this sentence wonderful alliteration.

00:15:07.210 --> 00:15:09.040
Listen to all these S'es.

00:15:09.040 --> 00:15:14.730
His features are said to bear striking, though
sable, resemblance to those of the president

00:15:14.730 --> 00:15:15.730
himself.

00:15:15.730 --> 00:15:17.990
It's like a hissing snake.

00:15:17.990 --> 00:15:19.850
This is well done stuff.

00:15:19.850 --> 00:15:25.110
Then, the rest of the piece, it rambles it
bit, and then finally Callender gets back

00:15:25.110 --> 00:15:26.790
in his stride at the very end.

00:15:26.790 --> 00:15:33.300
He says, "The allegations of a nature too
black to be suffered to remain in suspense.

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:37.750
We should be glad to hear of its refutation."

00:15:37.750 --> 00:15:40.360
Federalists were delighted with this story.

00:15:40.360 --> 00:15:41.550
They reprinted it.

00:15:41.550 --> 00:15:43.170
They wrote poems about it.

00:15:43.170 --> 00:15:45.870
They drew cartoons about it.

00:15:45.870 --> 00:15:51.590
One famous cartoon shows Jefferson and Sally
Hemings as a pair of chickens.

00:15:51.590 --> 00:15:58.310
Jefferson is the rooster, and the cartoon
is captioned a philosophic cock.

00:15:58.310 --> 00:16:00.400
They played rough in the 18th century.

00:16:00.400 --> 00:16:03.040
They really took the gloves off.

00:16:03.040 --> 00:16:08.230
Another audience for the story a little later
is Abolitionists.

00:16:08.230 --> 00:16:16.029
They tell the story to show that slavery is
such a wicked system that even the author

00:16:16.029 --> 00:16:22.620
of the Declaration of Independence, which
they revere, is caught in its toils and behaves

00:16:22.620 --> 00:16:23.620
badly.

00:16:23.620 --> 00:16:30.430
Then, a third audience who loved the story
were Englishmen, especially Englishmen visiting

00:16:30.430 --> 00:16:36.940
American and who were constantly told by Americans,
"Isn't this a great Democratic country, unlike

00:16:36.940 --> 00:16:39.631
you with all your kinds, and lords, and so
on."

00:16:39.631 --> 00:16:43.650
They liked using this story to turn it back
on Americans.

00:16:43.650 --> 00:16:50.300
Charles Dickens and the Irish poet, Thomas
Moore, both wrote about Jefferson dreaming

00:16:50.300 --> 00:16:54.529
of freedom in his slave's embrace.

00:16:54.529 --> 00:16:56.870
The story is still with us.

00:16:56.870 --> 00:17:03.720
When recent DNA tests showed that descendents
of Sally Hemings had a chromosome which is

00:17:03.720 --> 00:17:08.309
carried on the Jefferson male line.

00:17:08.309 --> 00:17:13.970
That's not 100% proof, but I think the burden
of proof does rest now with those would say

00:17:13.970 --> 00:17:16.610
that the story is false.

00:17:16.610 --> 00:17:22.350
We know about it not because of historians
or not even because of high-end classy journalists,

00:17:22.350 --> 00:17:28.250
but because of this attack journalist, James
Callender.

00:17:28.250 --> 00:17:34.540
For amusement, the founding father who we
rightly think as most amusing is Benjamin

00:17:34.540 --> 00:17:37.040
Franklin.

00:17:37.040 --> 00:17:41.000
Franklin could command many tones of voice.

00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:43.530
He could be the scientist.

00:17:43.530 --> 00:17:49.450
He could be giver of good advice, early to
bed or early to rise makes a man healthy,

00:17:49.450 --> 00:17:50.610
wealthy and wise.

00:17:50.610 --> 00:17:56.640
He had dozens of other sayings that he printed
in almanacs that we all know.

00:17:56.640 --> 00:18:00.480
We've seem to grow up knowing them.

00:18:00.480 --> 00:18:09.000
Franklin's maybe his best most effective voice
is humor, particularly in hoaxes. Franklin

00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:16.660
loves to print hoaxes, pretended essays pretended
as letters by people.

00:18:16.660 --> 00:18:22.530
He's the master of the straight face because
a hoax has to be done exactly right.

00:18:22.530 --> 00:18:26.000
If you push it too hard it's too much.

00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:31.710
If you don't catch the exact tone of what
you're imitating it won't work.

00:18:31.710 --> 00:18:39.250
The very last piece of journalism that Franklin
writes when he's an old man, almost 84 years

00:18:39.250 --> 00:18:40.720
old, is a hoax.

00:18:40.720 --> 00:18:47.170
It's his last hoax, and it's about slavery
and the slave trade.

00:18:47.170 --> 00:18:55.940
The Constitution, which has just gone into
effect in 1789, said that the slave trade

00:18:55.940 --> 00:19:00.380
could not be forbidden before 1808.

00:19:00.380 --> 00:19:04.650
This was the price of Georgia and South and
North Carolina signing.

00:19:04.650 --> 00:19:10.610
That's in the Constitution, and the Constitution
also said this provision may not be amended,

00:19:10.610 --> 00:19:16.450
so that was a firm deal that had been made.

00:19:16.450 --> 00:19:21.851
Nevertheless, Quakers submitted a petition
to the first Congress asking that the slave

00:19:21.851 --> 00:19:23.680
trade be ended.

00:19:23.680 --> 00:19:28.400
The Quaker religion had decided earlier in
the century that slavery was something no

00:19:28.400 --> 00:19:36.490
Christian could participate in and many Quakers
had become early abolitionists.

00:19:36.490 --> 00:19:41.340
They submitted a petition to Congress, and
they called the Constitutional Prohibition

00:19:41.340 --> 00:19:43.340
a seeming impediment.

00:19:43.340 --> 00:19:48.430
Congress, obviously, has to reject their petition.

00:19:48.430 --> 00:19:52.180
They don't have the power to accept it.

00:19:52.180 --> 00:19:58.490
One of the speeches on it is by representative
James Jackson of Georgia.

00:19:58.490 --> 00:20:03.510
He had some to this country when he was 15
years old in 1772.

00:20:03.510 --> 00:20:08.870
He'd fought in the Revolution, and now he
was a congressman.

00:20:08.870 --> 00:20:15.140
He criticizes the Quaker petition not just
on the grounds of its unconstitutionality,

00:20:15.140 --> 00:20:20.860
but he comes very close, no, he actually he
crosses the line.

00:20:20.860 --> 00:20:23.680
He defends slavery itself.

00:20:23.680 --> 00:20:29.300
This is what he said in Congress, "Why do
these men set themselves up in such a particular

00:20:29.300 --> 00:20:31.150
manner against slavery?

00:20:31.150 --> 00:20:36.330
Do they understand the rights of mankind and
the disposition of providence better than

00:20:36.330 --> 00:20:37.740
others?

00:20:37.740 --> 00:20:43.140
If they were to consult the Bible they will
find that slavery is not only allowed but

00:20:43.140 --> 00:20:44.730
commended.

00:20:44.730 --> 00:20:49.920
If they fully examine the subject they will
find that slavery has been no novel doctrine

00:20:49.920 --> 00:20:52.761
since the days of Cain."

00:20:52.761 --> 00:20:56.000
This was all Franklin needed to get going.

00:20:56.000 --> 00:21:01.680
He wrote a letter to a Philadephia newspaper.

00:21:01.680 --> 00:21:08.280
It begins this way, it's not obviously signed
Ben Franklin, it's signed Historicus, historian

00:21:08.280 --> 00:21:09.920
in Latin.

00:21:09.920 --> 00:21:17.260
"To the editor of the Federal Gazette, sir,
reading last night in your excellent paper

00:21:17.260 --> 00:21:23.700
the speech of Mr. Jackson in Congress against
meddling with the affair of slavery it put

00:21:23.700 --> 00:21:31.420
me in mind of a similar one made about 100
years since by Sidi Mohamed I brahim, a member

00:21:31.420 --> 00:21:37.870
of the Diwan of Algiers which may be seen
in Martin's Account of His Consulship published

00:21:37.870 --> 00:21:40.580
in 1687.

00:21:40.580 --> 00:21:46.890
It was against granting petition for the abolition
of piracy and slavery as being unjust.

00:21:46.890 --> 00:21:50.960
Mr. Jackson does not quote it, perhaps he
has not seen it.

00:21:50.960 --> 00:21:57.330
If, therefore, some of its reasonings to be
found in his eloquent speech it may only show

00:21:57.330 --> 00:22:03.490
that men's interests operate with surprising
similarly in all countries and climates whenever

00:22:03.490 --> 00:22:06.400
they are under similar circumstances.

00:22:06.400 --> 00:22:11.670
The African speech is translated is as follows:…"

00:22:11.670 --> 00:22:15.840
Before I get to the African speech, a couple
of points.

00:22:15.840 --> 00:22:17.550
First the tone.

00:22:17.550 --> 00:22:19.190
You all recognize the tone of this.

00:22:19.190 --> 00:22:22.370
This is a slightly pompous letter to the editor.

00:22:22.370 --> 00:22:26.710
We've all read those letters, maybe we've
written some of them.

00:22:26.710 --> 00:22:34.700
Here, he writes, he has a historical comparison,
he has the name, he has a book that you can

00:22:34.700 --> 00:22:37.560
look up.

00:22:37.560 --> 00:22:40.980
He says, yes, he gives the book.

00:22:40.980 --> 00:22:45.300
It's Martin's Account of His Consulship, so
evidently Martin, it sounds like an Englishman,

00:22:45.300 --> 00:22:51.211
an English diplomat who would've been a counsel
to Algiers, and he wrote this book in the

00:22:51.211 --> 00:22:52.880
17th century.

00:22:52.880 --> 00:22:57.770
After this letter was published, people went
to bookstores in Philadephia asking for Martin's

00:22:57.770 --> 00:22:59.780
Account of His Consulship.

00:22:59.780 --> 00:23:03.200
They didn't have much luck since there is
no such book.

00:23:03.200 --> 00:23:06.040
Then, Franklin goes on.

00:23:06.040 --> 00:23:10.790
He says, "Mr. Jackson does not quote it, perhaps
he has not seen it."

00:23:10.790 --> 00:23:12.640
Of course, he hasn't seen it.

00:23:12.640 --> 00:23:15.390
Benjamin Franklin is making it all up.

00:23:15.390 --> 00:23:23.270
Then, finally, he says, "The African's speech
as translated is as follows."

00:23:23.270 --> 00:23:29.700
Of course, that's a very subtle dig because
Algiers is northern African, so that is Africa,

00:23:29.700 --> 00:23:36.470
but sub-Saharan Africa is where all the slaves
of Georgia and of representative Jackson come

00:23:36.470 --> 00:23:37.520
from.

00:23:37.520 --> 00:23:43.760
He's kind of slyly equating Jackson's arguments
with African arguments.

00:23:43.760 --> 00:23:46.630
Here's the speech that he quotes.

00:23:46.630 --> 00:23:54.300
:The African speech as translated is as follows:
Allah Smallah, God is great and Mohammed is

00:23:54.300 --> 00:23:55.850
his prophet.

00:23:55.850 --> 00:24:03.210
How grossly are they mistaken in imagining
slavery to be disallowed by the al-Koran.

00:24:03.210 --> 00:24:08.772
Are not the two precepts, masters treat your
slaves with kindness, slaves serve with your

00:24:08.772 --> 00:24:14.490
masters with cheerfulness and fidelity, clear
proofs to the contrary?

00:24:14.490 --> 00:24:19.590
Nor can the plundering of infidels be in that
sacred book forbidden since it is well known

00:24:19.590 --> 00:24:25.110
from it that God has given the world and that
it contains to his faithful Musselmen who

00:24:25.110 --> 00:24:30.140
are to enjoy it of right as fast they can
conquer it.

00:24:30.140 --> 00:24:36.280
Let us then hear no more of this detestable
proposition."

00:24:36.280 --> 00:24:42.460
Franklin is not just drawing any comparison
from any part of the world here.

00:24:42.460 --> 00:24:47.010
He's talking about Algiers in the 17th century.

00:24:47.010 --> 00:24:50.240
Algiers is one of the Barbary states.

00:24:50.240 --> 00:24:56.911
These were four countries in north Africa,
Morocco, Algiers, Tunis, and Tripoli.

00:24:56.911 --> 00:25:03.960
In both the 17th and 18th century and into
the 19th century these countries lived by

00:25:03.960 --> 00:25:07.170
piracy and slavery.

00:25:07.170 --> 00:25:12.680
The arrangement was if you paid them every
year they would leave your shipping alone.

00:25:12.680 --> 00:25:16.239
You, meaning Christian countries of Europe.

00:25:16.239 --> 00:25:21.730
If you didn't, they would raid your ships
and sell everyone they captured into slavery.

00:25:21.730 --> 00:25:26.570
It was a religious extortion racket that they
ran.

00:25:26.570 --> 00:25:30.090
This is a current problem when Franklin is
writing.

00:25:30.090 --> 00:25:36.360
The United States would fight two wars with
the Barbary states early in the 19th century

00:25:36.360 --> 00:25:40.320
when Jefferson was president, then when Madison
was president.

00:25:40.320 --> 00:25:46.210
Franklin is writing 25 years before that,
so he's writing about an issue that is an

00:25:46.210 --> 00:25:50.010
ongoing international problem.

00:25:50.010 --> 00:25:56.620
Then, he ends, he quotes some more of this
speech and says, "The result was as Martin

00:25:56.620 --> 00:26:03.750
tells us that the Diwan came to the resolution
let the petition be rejected and it was rejected

00:26:03.750 --> 00:26:04.750
accordingly.

00:26:04.750 --> 00:26:10.590
I am, sir, your constant reader and humble
servant, Historicus."

00:26:10.590 --> 00:26:14.310
He's not just doing this to have fun.

00:26:14.310 --> 00:26:21.100
He's doing it to try and change people's perceptions.

00:26:21.100 --> 00:26:27.210
He's really saying if you can't see the evil
of slavery here because it's ours and it's

00:26:27.210 --> 00:26:36.480
so close to us, then look at it over there
where the slaves are Christian white men,

00:26:36.480 --> 00:26:39.250
then maybe you'll see the evil of it there.

00:26:39.250 --> 00:26:45.840
I don't know that it had any effect on representative
Jackson, but it did raise a laugh when it

00:26:45.840 --> 00:26:48.980
was printed in Philadelphia.

00:26:48.980 --> 00:26:54.240
For instruction, if we think of the founding
fathers in instruction we have to think of

00:26:54.240 --> 00:27:00.480
Alexander Hamilton because he is the great
know-it-all of the founding fathers.

00:27:00.480 --> 00:27:05.490
He not only believed that he was one of the
only people in the country who understood

00:27:05.490 --> 00:27:10.330
modern finance and economics, he was probably
right about that.

00:27:10.330 --> 00:27:15.360
He also believed that he understood everything
else, and he would be happy to tell you what

00:27:15.360 --> 00:27:20.740
he understood and how you could do your job
probably better than you were doing.

00:27:20.740 --> 00:27:25.720
I think this is one of the personal sources
of the first two-party system.

00:27:25.720 --> 00:27:27.450
It was about ideology.

00:27:27.450 --> 00:27:33.330
It was about issues, but frankly, Hamilton's
personality had a lot to do with it because

00:27:33.330 --> 00:27:39.250
some people appreciated his omniscience and
other people really disliked it including

00:27:39.250 --> 00:27:42.510
Thomas Jefferson.

00:27:42.510 --> 00:27:46.090
Hamilton was a journalist all his life.

00:27:46.090 --> 00:27:51.250
His first piece was written when he was 14
years old living in the Caribbean which is

00:27:51.250 --> 00:27:53.220
where he came from.

00:27:53.220 --> 00:28:00.390
A terrible hurricane hit St. Croix which was
where he was living at the time.

00:28:00.390 --> 00:28:09.190
He described it in a letter to his father,
and then it was published in a St. Croix newspaper.

00:28:09.190 --> 00:28:15.250
After it came out, two local figures decided
this is a bright young man we have to send

00:28:15.250 --> 00:28:17.870
him to the mainland to be educated.

00:28:17.870 --> 00:28:23.430
Hamilton was in effect an orphan at the time
because his mother had died, although he was

00:28:23.430 --> 00:28:30.270
writing his father, his father had taken off,
abandoned his family, just move elsewhere.

00:28:30.270 --> 00:28:37.669
The men who helped him were his boss, Hamilton
was a clerk in a counting house, and a local

00:28:37.669 --> 00:28:38.669
minister.

00:28:38.669 --> 00:28:41.830
The plan was to send him to the 13 colonies.

00:28:41.830 --> 00:28:48.309
He would become educated, and then he'd come
back to the islands as a doctor.

00:28:48.309 --> 00:28:50.630
The first plan was to send him to Princeton.

00:28:50.630 --> 00:28:56.130
That didn't work out, so he ended up going
to King's College which is now Columbia.

00:28:56.130 --> 00:29:02.210
He never graduated because the American Revolution
started, and he quit to fight in it, and then

00:29:02.210 --> 00:29:08.260
his career took off in the path that it would
follow.

00:29:08.260 --> 00:29:14.000
Hamilton continued to write journalism while
he was in college, while he was a soldier,

00:29:14.000 --> 00:29:20.240
and after the war when he starts getting into
law and into politics.

00:29:20.240 --> 00:29:26.370
The greatest of his projects was the Federalist
papers.

00:29:26.370 --> 00:29:31.110
The Constitution was, of course, discussed
and written in Philadelphia in the summer

00:29:31.110 --> 00:29:32.610
of 1787.

00:29:32.610 --> 00:29:41.820
When we think of debates over the Constitution
we often think of those debates in that hall.

00:29:41.820 --> 00:29:47.260
That's partly because we have a detailed account
of them left by James Madison.

00:29:47.260 --> 00:29:50.910
We have to remember that those debates were
secret.

00:29:50.910 --> 00:29:56.470
No one except the delegates knew anything
about them until years later.

00:29:56.470 --> 00:30:01.800
Madison's notes were not published until after
he died in 1840.

00:30:01.800 --> 00:30:08.419
The debates over the Constitution that mattered
were not those debates but the ones that occurred

00:30:08.419 --> 00:30:14.480
at the state ratifying conventions and in
the newspapers of the United States from the

00:30:14.480 --> 00:30:22.270
fall of 1787 to the fall of 1788 when the
Constitution is finally ratified.

00:30:22.270 --> 00:30:28.630
New York City, New York state, is a center
of opposition to the Constitution.

00:30:28.630 --> 00:30:31.580
New York sent three delegates to the convention.

00:30:31.580 --> 00:30:35.960
Hamilton was one of them, but the other two
left halfway through because they were disgusted

00:30:35.960 --> 00:30:38.560
with what was going on.

00:30:38.560 --> 00:30:44.790
One of them, Robert Yates, wrote a series
of anti-Constitution essays very quickly.

00:30:44.790 --> 00:30:48.920
He signed them Brutus, and he made a number
of powerful arguments.

00:30:48.920 --> 00:30:53.419
The first one was why is no bill of rights
in this Constitution.

00:30:53.419 --> 00:31:00.750
He made it very subtly because he says the
Constitution does guarantee habeas corpus

00:31:00.750 --> 00:31:04.039
and it forbids bills of attainder.

00:31:04.039 --> 00:31:10.300
If they put that in there why didn't they
put in freedom of religion, freedom of speech,

00:31:10.300 --> 00:31:12.220
no cruel and unusual punishments?

00:31:12.220 --> 00:31:15.600
Why are there none of other rights?

00:31:15.600 --> 00:31:21.490
He also makes a very power argument that republics
cannot be large.

00:31:21.490 --> 00:31:26.970
We can't have a national Republican government
in this country because it's already too big

00:31:26.970 --> 00:31:30.010
and his example is Rome.

00:31:30.010 --> 00:31:31.010
Look at Rome.

00:31:31.010 --> 00:31:36.820
Rome started as a city, but then it conquered
the whole Mediterranean, and at that point

00:31:36.820 --> 00:31:40.140
it changed from a republic to an empires.

00:31:40.140 --> 00:31:42.570
Republics have to be small.

00:31:42.570 --> 00:31:48.340
Alexander Hamilton who's now back in New York,
he sees the effect that Brutus is having and

00:31:48.340 --> 00:31:53.280
he knows the Constitution has to have a media
campaign in its support.

00:31:53.280 --> 00:31:56.450
Being Hamilton, he gets one going.

00:31:56.450 --> 00:32:04.230
He finds two collaborators, James Madison,
another delegate to the convention, and John

00:32:04.230 --> 00:32:09.530
Jay, the former diplomat and spymaster who's
an older man.

00:32:09.530 --> 00:32:13.610
There's five newspapers in New York City at
the time.

00:32:13.610 --> 00:32:17.100
Hamilton arranges for their essays to be run
in all five of them.

00:32:17.100 --> 00:32:20.770
He wanted to market saturation.

00:32:20.770 --> 00:32:24.740
The essays came out at the rate of four a
week.

00:32:24.740 --> 00:32:26.370
Some weeks there were five.

00:32:26.370 --> 00:32:28.990
One week there were six.

00:32:28.990 --> 00:32:32.210
These essays are each 2,000 words long.

00:32:32.210 --> 00:32:37.080
A typical op-ed piece today is 750 words.

00:32:37.080 --> 00:32:39.840
This was a brutal tempo.

00:32:39.840 --> 00:32:45.289
When Madison was an old man he remembered
that when we started writing these things

00:32:45.289 --> 00:32:51.770
we'd show our collaborators our first draft,
but it just became so rapid we couldn't do

00:32:51.770 --> 00:32:57.400
that anymore, so each man was in it for himself.

00:32:57.400 --> 00:33:02.690
Several times the printer's messenger would
be at someone's door while he was still finishing

00:33:02.690 --> 00:33:06.419
up a Federalist essay.

00:33:06.419 --> 00:33:09.340
Jay started off with four.

00:33:09.340 --> 00:33:11.060
Then, he got sick.

00:33:11.060 --> 00:33:15.320
Then, about two-thirds of the way through
he wrote another one, then he was injured

00:33:15.320 --> 00:33:19.440
in a riot, so he only ended up writing five.

00:33:19.440 --> 00:33:20.620
Madison wrote 29.

00:33:20.620 --> 00:33:25.820
Hamilton wrote 51 for 85 essays in all.

00:33:25.820 --> 00:33:29.310
They covered every aspect of the Constitution.

00:33:29.310 --> 00:33:32.680
They didn't quite answer the question of the
Bill of Rights.

00:33:32.680 --> 00:33:36.900
Hamilton wrote a rather interesting argument,
but it was not effectual.

00:33:36.900 --> 00:33:41.900
They did demolish the argument that republics
have to be small.

00:33:41.900 --> 00:33:47.270
Probably the most famous argument now is Madison's
where he says that in a large republic there

00:33:47.270 --> 00:33:52.360
will be so many conflicting interests that
no one of them will overwhelm all the others.

00:33:52.360 --> 00:33:58.120
Whereas, in a small one that would be easier
to happen.

00:33:58.120 --> 00:34:00.289
Hamilton also made an economic argument.

00:34:00.289 --> 00:34:07.960
He said that the 13 colonies naturally trade
with each other and profit from each other.

00:34:07.960 --> 00:34:11.619
Jay made maybe the strongest argument of all.

00:34:11.619 --> 00:34:19.630
He said, "If we have 13 states or three confederations
bordering each other they'll be constantly

00:34:19.630 --> 00:34:25.990
at war, and European powers will be pitting
them against each other and there'll be standing

00:34:25.990 --> 00:34:28.610
armies."

00:34:28.610 --> 00:34:31.480
Madison is making an argument from politics.

00:34:31.480 --> 00:34:34.240
Hamilton is making one from economics.

00:34:34.240 --> 00:34:37.990
Jay made one for peace.

00:34:37.990 --> 00:34:43.070
What I want to read you now is just from the
first paragraph of the first Federalist.

00:34:43.070 --> 00:34:44.500
It's Hamilton.

00:34:44.500 --> 00:34:47.179
This is about the whole project.

00:34:47.179 --> 00:34:53.440
It's really a statement of the best of Democratic
politics.

00:34:53.440 --> 00:35:00.760
Hamilton says, "To the people of the state
of New York, it seems to have been reserved

00:35:00.760 --> 00:35:07.840
to the people of this country to decide the
important question whether societies of men

00:35:07.840 --> 00:35:14.541
are really capable or not of estimating good
government from reflection and choice, or

00:35:14.541 --> 00:35:21.920
whether they are forever destined to depend
for their political constitutions on accident

00:35:21.920 --> 00:35:25.000
and force.

00:35:25.000 --> 00:35:33.349
Can we have good government from reflection
and choice, or must we forever depend on accident

00:35:33.349 --> 00:35:35.840
and force?"

00:35:35.840 --> 00:35:41.690
He's asking you to think of history, and he'll
go on to be very specific ticking off examples,

00:35:41.690 --> 00:35:43.760
but here he's just introducing it.

00:35:43.760 --> 00:35:52.500
Think of history, revolutions, assassinations,
civil wars.

00:35:52.500 --> 00:35:56.330
We could go through that again, but we can
do something different.

00:35:56.330 --> 00:36:00.900
We can reflect, and then we can choose.

00:36:00.900 --> 00:36:03.099
Then, he goes on.

00:36:03.099 --> 00:36:07.530
"I frankly acknowledge to you my convictions."

00:36:07.530 --> 00:36:11.990
That's the Hamilton note, I figured this out.

00:36:11.990 --> 00:36:17.990
Then he says, "I will freely lay before you
the reasons on which they are founded."

00:36:17.990 --> 00:36:20.770
That's also the Hamilton note.

00:36:20.770 --> 00:36:26.619
He was a know-it-all, but he never said agree
with me because I say so.

00:36:26.619 --> 00:36:33.640
He always said agree with me because here
are my reasons, I think it for this, and this,

00:36:33.640 --> 00:36:34.640
and this.

00:36:34.640 --> 00:36:37.450
He said, "I will tell you all my reasons.

00:36:37.450 --> 00:36:43.660
My friends and I will write 85 essays if you
want, and when we're done you will know everything

00:36:43.660 --> 00:36:45.300
I know."

00:36:45.300 --> 00:36:49.560
He was a know-it-all, but he was a very generous
know-it-all.

00:36:49.560 --> 00:36:55.670
That's the best statement in the spirit of
Democratic politics, trusting your electorate,

00:36:55.670 --> 00:37:02.369
trusting your audience, sharing with them
what you think, what you know.

00:37:02.369 --> 00:37:09.911
No, before I leave the Federalist papers,
of course, it had an effect in New York, probably

00:37:09.911 --> 00:37:11.050
also in Virginia.

00:37:11.050 --> 00:37:13.640
It was re-printed there.

00:37:13.640 --> 00:37:19.770
Maybe one of the best judgments on it, the
Marquis de Talleyrand, who was wily French

00:37:19.770 --> 00:37:23.600
diplomat, he had to live for two years in
the United States.

00:37:23.600 --> 00:37:29.109
France got a little hot for him, so he took
a little sabbatical in the United States where

00:37:29.109 --> 00:37:32.170
one of his friends was Hamilton whom he greatly
admired.

00:37:32.170 --> 00:37:40.830
Then, when he was back in France running things
there from behind the scenes a Spanish diplomat

00:37:40.830 --> 00:37:44.720
remarked to him that he had never read the
Federalist papers.

00:37:44.720 --> 00:37:49.410
Talleyrand, the old fox, just said, "Read
it.

00:37:49.410 --> 00:37:51.270
Read it."

00:37:51.270 --> 00:37:55.000
Finally, inspiration.

00:37:55.000 --> 00:37:59.720
The journalist who exemplifies is Thomas Payne.

00:37:59.720 --> 00:38:01.490
Payne is another immigrant.

00:38:01.490 --> 00:38:06.990
You will see that three of these four people
are immigrants, and I think that's because

00:38:06.990 --> 00:38:10.900
journalism pulls for outsiders.

00:38:10.900 --> 00:38:12.810
Payne is an Englishman.

00:38:12.810 --> 00:38:15.470
He had a various life in England.

00:38:15.470 --> 00:38:16.660
He was a corset maker.

00:38:16.660 --> 00:38:18.260
He was a tax collector.

00:38:18.260 --> 00:38:20.530
He was a privateer.

00:38:20.530 --> 00:38:29.609
Then, in 1774, he comes to the United States
and there he finds himself as a journalist.

00:38:29.609 --> 00:38:36.830
His first great his it s pamphlet in 1776
Common Sense.

00:38:36.830 --> 00:38:40.880
This is an argument for American declaring
independence.

00:38:40.880 --> 00:38:47.180
We've already been fighting Britain for eight
months, but we've done it still professing

00:38:47.180 --> 00:38:48.490
loyalty to the king.

00:38:48.490 --> 00:38:53.510
It's only are king's ministers we say are
fighting.

00:38:53.510 --> 00:38:57.300
Payne writes Common Sense to say it's past
that point.

00:38:57.300 --> 00:38:58.960
It's really past that point.

00:38:58.960 --> 00:39:02.560
We should declare our independence.

00:39:02.560 --> 00:39:07.770
His greatest moment comes at the end of that
year, in December of 1776.

00:39:07.770 --> 00:39:15.160
The second half of that year was a disaster
for the American cause.

00:39:15.160 --> 00:39:21.440
The British decided to attack New York, make
that their base.

00:39:21.440 --> 00:39:24.830
Washington loses the battle of Long Island
in Brooklyn.

00:39:24.830 --> 00:39:31.080
Then, the British land on Manhattan, they
drive him steadily off the island.

00:39:31.080 --> 00:39:34.770
He loses the battle of White Plains in October.

00:39:34.770 --> 00:39:38.170
He's left one garrison in northern Manhattan,
Fort Washington.

00:39:38.170 --> 00:39:40.460
It's now Inwood Park.

00:39:40.460 --> 00:39:43.150
There are 3,000 men in that fortress.

00:39:43.150 --> 00:39:47.440
Nathaniel Greene tells Washington it can be
defended, but Greene is wrong.

00:39:47.440 --> 00:39:51.440
The British take it and all 3,000 men.

00:39:51.440 --> 00:39:56.270
Washington's watching this across the Hudson
River in New Jersey and one man said he wept

00:39:56.270 --> 00:39:59.920
like a child when he saw what was happening.

00:39:59.920 --> 00:40:04.990
The British cross the Hudson, pursued him
through New Jersey.

00:40:04.990 --> 00:40:06.839
He was loosing soldiers.

00:40:06.839 --> 00:40:09.990
His soldiers had no shoes, no adequate clothing.

00:40:09.990 --> 00:40:16.130
You could follow the line of his retreat by
the blood of their footprints.

00:40:16.130 --> 00:40:21.990
This won gloriously with the victories of
Trenton and Princeton at the end of the year,

00:40:21.990 --> 00:40:26.300
but they didn't know that in the middle of
December.

00:40:26.300 --> 00:40:28.970
Thomas Payne is living in Philadelphia.

00:40:28.970 --> 00:40:35.000
He comes to the army in New Jersey to follow
it and report on its progress.

00:40:35.000 --> 00:40:36.119
He writes and essay.

00:40:36.119 --> 00:40:42.460
He writes it on a drumhead by campfire light,
then he goes back to Philadelphia to get it

00:40:42.460 --> 00:40:43.460
printed.

00:40:43.460 --> 00:40:47.490
It's called The American Crisis.

00:40:47.490 --> 00:40:51.150
It has, I think, the best lead in the history
of journalism.

00:40:51.150 --> 00:40:56.390
Here it is, "These are the time that try men's
souls.

00:40:56.390 --> 00:41:02.410
The summer soldier and the sunshine patriot
will in this crisis shrink from the service

00:41:02.410 --> 00:41:10.240
of their country, but he that stands it now
deserves the love and thanks of man and woman.

00:41:10.240 --> 00:41:16.950
Tyranny like hell is not easily conquered,
yet we have this consolation with us that

00:41:16.950 --> 00:41:21.730
the harder the conflict the more glorious
the triumph.

00:41:21.730 --> 00:41:26.660
What we obtain too cheap we esteem too lightly.

00:41:26.660 --> 00:41:31.349
It is dearness only that gives everything
it's value.

00:41:31.349 --> 00:41:37.710
Heaven knows how to put a proper price upon
it's goods and it will strange indeed if so

00:41:37.710 --> 00:41:43.869
celestial an article as freedom should not
be highly rated."

00:41:43.869 --> 00:41:49.010
I don't know if Payne was thinking of Shakespeare
when he wrote that.

00:41:49.010 --> 00:41:57.400
I think of King Harry's speech before the
Battle of Agincourt and Henry V where he says

00:41:57.400 --> 00:42:03.240
this band, this happy band, this band of brothers
and every man that fights with this day shall

00:42:03.240 --> 00:42:07.460
gentle his condition, shall become a gentleman.

00:42:07.460 --> 00:42:11.589
Payne is writing the Republican equivalent
of that.

00:42:11.589 --> 00:42:15.330
You're not going to become gentleman for doing
this.

00:42:15.330 --> 00:42:20.630
You're going to earn the love and the thanks
of man and woman, of your neighbors, of your

00:42:20.630 --> 00:42:22.619
peers.

00:42:22.619 --> 00:42:24.730
I don't know what to say about this.

00:42:24.730 --> 00:42:25.730
This is so eloquent.

00:42:25.730 --> 00:42:29.760
I don't see how it can possibly be improved.

00:42:29.760 --> 00:42:31.599
The peace goes on.

00:42:31.599 --> 00:42:37.810
It doesn't maintain quite that level, how
could it, but there is another very moving

00:42:37.810 --> 00:42:44.700
moment towards the end where Payne is talking
about a Tory, an opponent of the revolution

00:42:44.700 --> 00:42:48.100
who's a tavern keeper in New Jersey.

00:42:48.100 --> 00:42:53.350
He says, "He was standing at his door with
as a pretty a child in his hand, about eight

00:42:53.350 --> 00:42:57.589
or nine years old, as I ever saw.

00:42:57.589 --> 00:43:04.099
After speaking his mind finished with this
unfatherly expression, well, give me peace

00:43:04.099 --> 00:43:06.510
in my day."

00:43:06.510 --> 00:43:09.310
He's saying why are we having this revolution.

00:43:09.310 --> 00:43:10.310
It's not worth it.

00:43:10.310 --> 00:43:11.760
Give me peace in my day.

00:43:11.760 --> 00:43:19.600
Then, Payne says, "A generous parent should've
said, 'If there must be trouble, let be in

00:43:19.600 --> 00:43:24.710
my day that my child may have peace.'"

00:43:24.710 --> 00:43:31.319
Payne is a little wrong because, of course,
children will all have their troubles, but

00:43:31.319 --> 00:43:36.310
he's right because let's not pass ours to
our children.

00:43:36.310 --> 00:43:43.600
If we have to fight to solve them let's do
that.

00:43:43.600 --> 00:43:47.480
What became of these men, the journalists?

00:43:47.480 --> 00:43:53.910
The first to die was Benjamin Franklin, 1790,
just some months after his little hoax at

00:43:53.910 --> 00:43:56.910
representative Jackson's expense.

00:43:56.910 --> 00:44:01.150
He was covered with honors and everybody loved
him.

00:44:01.150 --> 00:44:05.250
The second one to die was James Callender.

00:44:05.250 --> 00:44:11.620
Ten months after wrote about Sally Hemings,
he drowned in the James River in three feet

00:44:11.620 --> 00:44:15.280
of water.

00:44:15.280 --> 00:44:22.210
If you were insulted in those days by a gentleman
you could challenge him to a duel.

00:44:22.210 --> 00:44:26.410
Dueling was illegal everywhere, but it was
never prosecuted because juries would not

00:44:26.410 --> 00:44:28.390
convict.

00:44:28.390 --> 00:44:32.350
Journalists were mostly not gentleman, so
you didn't challenge them to duels.

00:44:32.350 --> 00:44:34.650
You could beat them up.

00:44:34.650 --> 00:44:41.160
Payne had been beaten with a club a few weeks
before that by a son-in-law of James Monroe.

00:44:41.160 --> 00:44:48.530
The coroner's jury ruled that his drowning
was accidental.

00:44:48.530 --> 00:44:52.800
The next one to die was Alexander Hamilton
in 1804.

00:44:52.800 --> 00:44:54.550
He did die in a duel.

00:44:54.550 --> 00:44:59.270
He was shot and killed by the Vice-President
of the United States.

00:44:59.270 --> 00:45:05.610
Journalism involved in it because a letter
had appeared in an upstate newspaper about

00:45:05.610 --> 00:45:12.830
a dinner party of Federalists, and the letter
writer said there was some criticism of Vice-Present

00:45:12.830 --> 00:45:21.560
Burr, and Hamilton said that he could give
a still more despicable opinion of him.

00:45:21.560 --> 00:45:24.580
Hamilton and Burr had been fighting politically
for years.

00:45:24.580 --> 00:45:29.720
They were both New Yorkers, both lawyers,
they knew each other.

00:45:29.720 --> 00:45:33.090
Burr always seemed worse to Hamilton than
Jefferson.

00:45:33.090 --> 00:45:36.810
I think in a funny way he trusted Jefferson.

00:45:36.810 --> 00:45:39.970
He knew what Jefferson would do.

00:45:39.970 --> 00:45:44.849
From his point of view most of it would be
wrong, but Jefferson was predictable.

00:45:44.849 --> 00:45:50.420
He thought Jefferson was honest, and he thought
Jefferson was a cautious man.

00:45:50.420 --> 00:45:55.580
From Hamilton's point of view Jefferson would
always talk a worse game than he would do.

00:45:55.580 --> 00:45:57.250
Burr, he never trusted.

00:45:57.250 --> 00:46:02.790
He thought there was something Napoleonic
about Burr, so there was a different edge

00:46:02.790 --> 00:46:05.750
to the things he said about him.

00:46:05.750 --> 00:46:09.190
Burr reads this letter in the upstate newspaper.

00:46:09.190 --> 00:46:11.670
He asks for an explanation.

00:46:11.670 --> 00:46:16.359
The two gentleman met a dueling ground in
New Jersey.

00:46:16.359 --> 00:46:18.339
Hamilton shot, missed.

00:46:18.339 --> 00:46:22.570
Burr hit Hamilton in liver and lodged in his
spine.

00:46:22.570 --> 00:46:28.359
Took him 36 hours to die, and I've seen the
front page of the New York Post of that evening,

00:46:28.359 --> 00:46:31.349
and there's a little box dropped in the front
page.

00:46:31.349 --> 00:46:35.730
It's like a news flash or the crawler on a
CNN screen.

00:46:35.730 --> 00:46:40.830
It says Hamilton injured in a duel, and the
next day he died.

00:46:40.830 --> 00:46:45.830
The last one to die was Thomas Payne.

00:46:45.830 --> 00:46:51.660
After our revolution, he goes to France and
gets involved in its.

00:46:51.660 --> 00:46:58.260
He's elected to the French National Assembly
even though he hardly speaks any French.

00:46:58.260 --> 00:47:04.130
He votes to depose Louis XVI and to set up
a republic.

00:47:04.130 --> 00:47:12.020
He argues against executing him, and his argument
is that Louis XVI has grown up in a royal

00:47:12.020 --> 00:47:17.200
world, what else did he ever know, we should
send him to America and he can live under

00:47:17.200 --> 00:47:21.820
a republican government and maybe he'll improve
as a person.

00:47:21.820 --> 00:47:24.970
This argument is not persuasive.

00:47:24.970 --> 00:47:27.640
Payne was arrested.

00:47:27.640 --> 00:47:30.170
He was slated to for execution.

00:47:30.170 --> 00:47:34.440
The only reason he was not guillotined is
that they passed his cell by accident the

00:47:34.440 --> 00:47:36.589
day he was to be killed.

00:47:36.589 --> 00:47:42.590
Then, there's a change in the wheel of politics
and he's released.

00:47:42.590 --> 00:47:45.359
He comes back to America in 1802.

00:47:45.359 --> 00:47:53.190
By this time, he's drinking heavily and he
dies poor in Greenwich Village in 1809.

00:47:53.190 --> 00:47:56.860
Journalism can do great things.

00:47:56.860 --> 00:47:59.730
Journalists don't necessarily have great lives.

00:47:59.730 --> 00:48:04.990
Thank you very much.

00:48:04.990 --> 00:48:17.480
Speaker 2: We have a few minutes for questions.

00:48:17.480 --> 00:48:19.119
We have microphones coming thoughout.

00:48:19.119 --> 00:48:22.520
Please raise your hand and stand.

00:48:22.520 --> 00:48:30.560
Also, please speak up and keep the question
shorter than the answer might be.

00:48:30.560 --> 00:48:36.530
Let me also mention that right after the Q&amp;A
session, Mr. Brookhiser has agreed to sign

00:48:36.530 --> 00:48:42.750
copies of the Founder's Son: A Life of Abraham
Lincoln, his newest book, over here just on

00:48:42.750 --> 00:48:45.250
this level by the staircase.

00:48:45.250 --> 00:48:50.900
Richard: Okay, any questions?

00:48:50.900 --> 00:48:53.619
Great, I've explained it all.

00:48:53.619 --> 00:48:54.660
Yes, there we go.

00:48:54.660 --> 00:48:55.660
Yes, sir.

00:48:55.660 --> 00:49:05.320
Audience: Regarding Dr. Franklin, I've read
where he wrote a fictitious letter which was

00:49:05.320 --> 00:49:13.310
his style, all the way from Silence Dogood,
called the Tobacco Letter in which he used

00:49:13.310 --> 00:49:21.320
psychological warfare to convince Hessians
to desert.

00:49:21.320 --> 00:49:24.170
So far from what I've read, this is all speculation.

00:49:24.170 --> 00:49:31.800
I'd like your comment if indeed this was written
by Dr. Franklin or not.

00:49:31.800 --> 00:49:41.670
It has his fingerprints all over it and to
divulge the actual author would absolutely

00:49:41.670 --> 00:49:47.390
nullify any good that it would do as far as
the Hessians deserting.

00:49:47.390 --> 00:49:51.800
Please …
Richard: Look, he wrote so many of these hoaxes

00:49:51.800 --> 00:49:55.140
I cannot keep track of them.

00:49:55.140 --> 00:49:59.780
All his works are published, so you can look
in there.

00:49:59.780 --> 00:50:03.840
He did keep it up during the Revolution.

00:50:03.840 --> 00:50:06.870
He was sent to France as a diplomat.

00:50:06.870 --> 00:50:13.349
He lived outside of Paris, but he set up a
printing press in his house where he churned

00:50:13.349 --> 00:50:18.550
out articles and some of them are humorous
pieces, but he also wrote political pieces,

00:50:18.550 --> 00:50:24.800
a tax on British conduct and also hoaxes of
the kind you're talking about.

00:50:24.800 --> 00:50:26.400
It certainly sounds like him.

00:50:26.400 --> 00:50:33.240
I simply don't know, but the complete works
are out there, so not so hard to chase down.

00:50:33.240 --> 00:50:34.830
Yes, sir.

00:50:34.830 --> 00:50:37.210
There we go.

00:50:37.210 --> 00:50:40.990
Audience: Sir, thank you very much for your
time today.

00:50:40.990 --> 00:50:45.089
My question's going to be going back to your
talk about James Callender and that type of

00:50:45.089 --> 00:50:46.089
investigative reporting.

00:50:46.089 --> 00:50:48.400
How does that relate to contemporary press
coverage?

00:50:48.400 --> 00:50:53.470
If you could just expand a little bit on that
from the evolution of freedom of the press

00:50:53.470 --> 00:50:56.480
from that period in history into today's modern
era.

00:50:56.480 --> 00:50:58.390
Thank you, sir.

00:50:58.390 --> 00:51:03.330
Audience: I mean look, there are obvious similarities.

00:51:03.330 --> 00:51:10.329
Callender, his two big scoops involved sex
scandals, the one he elicited from Hamilton,

00:51:10.329 --> 00:51:16.730
and then other he simply tried to tie to Jefferson's
tail.

00:51:16.730 --> 00:51:20.200
Heaven knows, we wallow in this stuff.

00:51:20.200 --> 00:51:27.540
The Clinton era sort of opened the flood gates
and on and on it goes.

00:51:27.540 --> 00:51:32.280
There is something disreputable about journalism,
there is.

00:51:32.280 --> 00:51:35.460
It's always there, and it will never go away.

00:51:35.460 --> 00:51:42.339
John, I hope I'm not corrupting your students,
but this is an aspect of it.

00:51:42.339 --> 00:51:49.730
Journalists tell things that people don't
want known, although we like to read them.

00:51:49.730 --> 00:51:55.690
A lot of those things are misbehavior.

00:51:55.690 --> 00:51:58.780
This is just in the mix.

00:51:58.780 --> 00:52:01.790
It's never going to disappear.

00:52:01.790 --> 00:52:07.950
Journalists, David Brooks, he recently was
on some TV show and he talked about there's

00:52:07.950 --> 00:52:12.500
the adult table and the kiddie table of journalism.

00:52:12.500 --> 00:52:14.030
David's an old friend of mine.

00:52:14.030 --> 00:52:15.610
I thought, "Oh geez, David.

00:52:15.610 --> 00:52:17.040
Come on.

00:52:17.040 --> 00:52:22.220
The kiddies all over the place, and somehow
adults behave like them, and sometimes the

00:52:22.220 --> 00:52:25.880
kiddies grow up."

00:52:25.880 --> 00:52:30.260
That's journalism and it's never going go
change it's spots entirely.

00:52:30.260 --> 00:52:36.040
Audience: Thank you, Mr. Brookhiser.

00:52:36.040 --> 00:52:40.990
The four aspects that you mentioned, are there
journalists today or in the recent past that

00:52:40.990 --> 00:52:49.099
you would cite as examples as amusing, inspiring,
attacking, instructional?

00:52:49.099 --> 00:52:56.160
Richard: I'm just going to talk about the
18th century.

00:52:56.160 --> 00:52:58.329
Sorry.

00:52:58.329 --> 00:53:07.860
Audience: Speaking of instructive, James Madison
certainly was in that category not only because

00:53:07.860 --> 00:53:13.710
he collaborated with Hamilton in the Federalist,
but because he and Hamilton were writing dueling

00:53:13.710 --> 00:53:15.210
essays in the 1790s.

00:53:15.210 --> 00:53:17.790
Richard: Be careful how you use the word dueling.

00:53:17.790 --> 00:53:19.290
Audience: Yeah, yeah.

00:53:19.290 --> 00:53:20.290
Journalist dueling.

00:53:20.290 --> 00:53:21.290
Richard: Right.

00:53:21.290 --> 00:53:25.850
Audience: In any event, which was quite an
informative period.

00:53:25.850 --> 00:53:28.410
Hamilton started the National Gazette.

00:53:28.410 --> 00:53:35.579
I'm sorry, the Gazette of the United States
which John Fenn was the editor, and then Madison

00:53:35.579 --> 00:53:40.040
and Hamilton countered with the National Gazette.

00:53:40.040 --> 00:53:46.320
That was war going on early in the administration,
well through the Washington administrations.

00:53:46.320 --> 00:53:50.210
I'd just like you to comment on the significance
of that.

00:53:50.210 --> 00:53:59.130
Audience: Doris Kerns Goodwin wrote this very
good book about the Lincoln administration

00:53:59.130 --> 00:54:02.720
which she called team of rivals.

00:54:02.720 --> 00:54:07.740
That phrase has become like management mantra,
hasn't it?

00:54:07.740 --> 00:54:10.720
You just see a lot.

00:54:10.720 --> 00:54:13.910
People think of that, that's the way to run
my company.

00:54:13.910 --> 00:54:18.150
I should get four or five people who hate
each other and completely disagree and then

00:54:18.150 --> 00:54:19.770
it'll be great.

00:54:19.770 --> 00:54:26.990
The first team of rivals was the Washington
administration.

00:54:26.990 --> 00:54:28.940
It was not supposed to be that way.

00:54:28.940 --> 00:54:30.859
No one thought it would be that way.

00:54:30.859 --> 00:54:37.690
It only became so over time and partly because
of Hamilton's personality, partly because

00:54:37.690 --> 00:54:39.800
the French Revolution happened.

00:54:39.800 --> 00:54:45.780
Washington is inaugurated in April of 1789
and the Bastille falls in July.

00:54:45.780 --> 00:54:52.980
When he's first thinking of his cabinet this
is not on his table, and then, just the issues

00:54:52.980 --> 00:54:56.079
of setting up a government and how it should
be set up.

00:54:56.079 --> 00:54:58.890
It was hell for all of them.

00:54:58.890 --> 00:55:01.520
It was just hell for them.

00:55:01.520 --> 00:55:07.970
It's a tribute to Washington's leadership
and to his skill that he kept these guys in

00:55:07.970 --> 00:55:11.470
the same tent as long as he did.

00:55:11.470 --> 00:55:18.930
He worked to keep Thomas Jefferson in his
job as Secretary of State long after Jefferson

00:55:18.930 --> 00:55:25.410
had turned against the Washington admission
and was subsidizing this newspaper which attacked

00:55:25.410 --> 00:55:27.510
the Washington administration.

00:55:27.510 --> 00:55:34.160
Washington figured keep your enemies close,
and so he worked and worked.

00:55:34.160 --> 00:55:36.210
Finally, Jefferson left, but it was it was
brilliant job.

00:55:36.210 --> 00:55:39.880
He kept him on much longer than you would've
thought.

00:55:39.880 --> 00:55:43.640
Also, this was all new.

00:55:43.640 --> 00:55:51.070
I think one reason for the frenzy of politics
in the 1790s, some historian called it the

00:55:51.070 --> 00:55:56.010
age of passion is that it was brand new.

00:55:56.010 --> 00:56:04.420
Now we know if you lose you're going to have
another chance in four year or eight years.

00:56:04.420 --> 00:56:11.710
No party is ever run out of town, but they
didn't know that because this was brand new.

00:56:11.710 --> 00:56:18.329
They knew the Constitution they had written,
and they knew the laws that they had passed,

00:56:18.329 --> 00:56:24.109
but you don't believe in them until you have
experience of them.

00:56:24.109 --> 00:56:31.010
Federalists truly thought that the Republicans
might set up guillotines if they want.

00:56:31.010 --> 00:56:35.250
The Republicans truly thought that Hamilton
was a monarchist.

00:56:35.250 --> 00:56:38.970
Those are both nonsensical.

00:56:38.970 --> 00:56:42.280
Thomas Jefferson was not going to set up guillotines.

00:56:42.280 --> 00:56:47.360
He praised them in France, but he had no intention
of setting them up here.

00:56:47.360 --> 00:56:49.980
Hamilton was not a monarchist.

00:56:49.980 --> 00:56:55.060
He fought against a monarchy in the Revolution.

00:56:55.060 --> 00:56:58.730
Both sides had these misapprehensions of the
other.

00:56:58.730 --> 00:57:04.570
It's really, then of course, we did have the
Civil War in the 1860s, but it takes a lot

00:57:04.570 --> 00:57:11.890
of experience before a country realizes that
it's unlikely that it will tear itself apart,

00:57:11.890 --> 00:57:17.600
and it's unlikely that the winners will simply
crush the losers.

00:57:17.600 --> 00:57:18.600
That was something.

00:57:18.600 --> 00:57:21.340
It was even than wisdom it took and even more
than genius.

00:57:21.340 --> 00:57:27.340
It took experience to live that lesson.

00:57:27.340 --> 00:57:28.340
One more?

00:57:28.340 --> 00:57:29.590
Speaker 2: We have time for one more question.

00:57:29.590 --> 00:57:37.510
Audience: You mentioned earlier in your speech
that back then the newspapers were all clearly

00:57:37.510 --> 00:57:43.570
biased and everybody knew that every newspaper
they read had a point of view.

00:57:43.570 --> 00:57:51.619
What do you think about the strengths and
weaknesses of that system and how effective

00:57:51.619 --> 00:57:52.619
it was compared to what we have today?

00:57:52.619 --> 00:57:53.619
Richard: I kind of like it.

00:57:53.619 --> 00:57:55.710
It's more straightforward.

00:57:55.710 --> 00:57:57.900
The newspapers did have information.

00:57:57.900 --> 00:58:01.849
It wasn't screeds all the time.

00:58:01.849 --> 00:58:05.369
They would report on the shipping news, and
they'd tell you about the battles in Europe,

00:58:05.369 --> 00:58:11.660
and they'd report the doings of Congress,
and that's also a selling point, right?

00:58:11.660 --> 00:58:14.390
That's interesting information that's useful.

00:58:14.390 --> 00:58:17.750
Your readers want to know it, you want to
supply it.

00:58:17.750 --> 00:58:21.089
They did get the information out and the news
out.

00:58:21.089 --> 00:58:29.370
Then, they had their point of view, and people
knew what it was and expected it.

00:58:29.370 --> 00:58:33.890
I the system worked pretty well.

00:58:33.890 --> 00:58:38.950
I think I've indicated that the lows of journalism
were lower than they are now.

00:58:38.950 --> 00:58:43.820
They really were, but the highs were higher.

00:58:43.820 --> 00:58:46.220
We had Thomas Payne at his best.

00:58:46.220 --> 00:58:51.950
The Federalist papers were running as op-eds,
and I think that's a pretty good credit to

00:58:51.950 --> 00:58:53.210
the founding era.

00:58:53.210 --> 00:58:54.560
Okay, thank you very much.

